1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:02,990
This is a cheetah at full sprint, but

2
00:00:02,990 --> 00:00:06,470
slow it down and something strange appears. Every

3
00:00:06,470 --> 00:00:09,360
video is just a stack of flat pictures.

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,150
Stack them one behind another and time becomes

5
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:14,790
a direction you can point to. Playing the

6
00:00:14,790 --> 00:00:17,430
video is simply sliding a flat slice through

7
00:00:17,430 --> 00:00:20,040
a solid block. Now take a cube. We

8
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:22,630
only ever see its flat shadow, yet our

9
00:00:22,630 --> 00:00:25,710
brains understand it instantly. So if a cube

10
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:29,770
casts a two-dimensional shadow, what casts a three-dimensional

11
00:00:29,770 --> 00:00:32,980
one? An object from the fourth dimension, and

12
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:35,360
this is what its shadow looks like. By

13
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:37,100
the end of this video, you will not

14
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:40,240
just see this shape. You will understand it.

15
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:42,470
Using vectors and a little linear algebra, we

16
00:00:42,470 --> 00:00:45,710
will count its corners, measure its diagonal, slice

17
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:47,410
it, and rotate it in a way that

18
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:50,820
is impossible in our world. So first, what

19
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:54,030
actually is a dimension? Imagine an ant on

20
00:00:54,030 --> 00:00:56,160
a wire. To say where it is, you

21
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:58,960
need one number, how far along the wire,

22
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:01,460
one dimension. Put the ant on a table

23
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:03,710
and you need two, how far right, and

24
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:06,150
how far forward. Let it fly into the

25
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:08,640
room and you need a third, how high.

26
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:11,300
So a dimension is simply the number of

27
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:14,120
independent numbers you need to describe a position.

28
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:16,580
We write those numbers as a vector. The

29
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:19,770
vector three to five means three steps along

30
00:01:19,770 --> 00:01:22,920
x, two along y, and five along z.

31
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:25,580
Each direction has its own unit arrow, called

32
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:27,600
a basis vector, and every point is just

33
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:30,150
a mix of those arrows. Now here is

34
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:32,900
the trick. Nothing forces us to stop at

35
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:35,410
three. Add a fourth number, w, and a

36
00:01:35,410 --> 00:01:37,990
fourth arrow, at right angles to x, y,

37
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:40,710
and z all at once. You cannot picture

38
00:01:40,710 --> 00:01:42,700
where it points, but you can add it,

39
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:45,130
scale it, and measure it exactly like the

40
00:01:45,130 --> 00:01:48,120
others. The algebra does not even flinch. You

41
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:50,610
have already seen a space like this. Three

42
00:01:50,610 --> 00:01:53,080
pixel in the Cheetah video needs three numbers.

43
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:55,880
It's x, it's y, and it's frame. The

44
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:58,840
video is a three-dimensional block, where the third

45
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:01,560
direction is time. Add one more number, and

46
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:04,010
you have four dimensions. So let's build a

47
00:02:04,010 --> 00:02:07,670
four-dimensional object with one simple move. Drag a

48
00:02:07,670 --> 00:02:10,300
shape along a new direction. Drag a point

49
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:12,340
along x, and you get a line segment

50
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:14,880
with two ends. Drag the segment along y,

51
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:17,240
and you get a square with four corners.

52
00:02:17,270 --> 00:02:19,340
Drag the square along z, and you get

53
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:21,820
a cube with eight corners. Now drag the

54
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:24,720
cube along w, the direction we cannot see.

55
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:28,290
The result is a tesseract, a hypercube. Every

56
00:02:28,290 --> 00:02:31,160
drag copies the shape, so the corners double.

57
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:35,080
One, two, four, eight, sixteen. A cube in

58
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:37,670
end dimensions has two to the power n

59
00:02:37,670 --> 00:02:40,890
corners. Edges follow a pattern, too. You keep

60
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:43,060
the edges of both copies and add one

61
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:45,450
new edge for every corner that moved. A

62
00:02:45,450 --> 00:02:48,520
cube has two times four plus four, which

63
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:51,690
is twelve edges. A tesseract has two times

64
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:55,240
twelve plus eight, which is thirty-two edges. It

65
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:58,600
also has twenty-four square faces and eight cubes

66
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:01,930
as its walls. In coordinates, every corner is

67
00:03:01,930 --> 00:03:05,230
made of zeros and ones. Zero, zero, zero,

68
00:03:05,230 --> 00:03:08,360
zero is one corner. One, one, one, one

69
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:12,840
is the opposite corner. Sixteen combinations, sixteen corners.

70
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:16,840
Third we can measure it. By Pythagoras, the

71
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:18,140
diagonal of a unit square is the square

72
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:19,410
root of one plus one, which is the

73
00:03:19,410 --> 00:03:21,790
square root of two. A cube adds one

74
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:24,720
more term, giving the square root of three.

75
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:27,600
A tesseract adds one more, giving the square

76
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:30,910
root of four. Exactly two. The length of

77
00:03:30,910 --> 00:03:32,860
any vector is the square root of the

78
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:34,890
sum of its squared parts, whether it has

79
00:03:34,890 --> 00:03:37,900
two parts or two thousand. But how would

80
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:41,230
we actually see a four-dimensional object? Only in

81
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:44,660
pieces. To understand why, shrink down by one

82
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:47,250
dimension. Imagine a flat creature living on a

83
00:03:47,250 --> 00:03:49,740
sheet of paper. A ball falls through its

84
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:52,360
world. The creature never sees a ball. It

85
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:55,080
sees a dot appear, growing to a circle,

86
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:58,310
shrink, and vanish. Each moment is one slice

87
00:03:58,310 --> 00:04:00,980
of the sphere. One level up, a four-dimensional

88
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:03,120
ball passing through our space would look like

89
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:06,700
a tiny sphere appearing from nowhere, swelling, shrinking,

90
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:09,540
and disappearing. That is exactly what playing the

91
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:12,120
Cheetah video did. Each frame was one slice

92
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:15,680
of a three-dimensional block. Slices of a tesseract

93
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:18,400
are even stranger. Push it through corner first,

94
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:20,380
and you first see a tiny tetrahedron, a

95
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:24,290
triangular pyramid. It grows, its corners get clipped,

96
00:04:24,290 --> 00:04:26,310
and at the middle it becomes a perfect

97
00:04:26,310 --> 00:04:29,800
octahedron, like two pyramids glued base to base.

98
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:32,760
Then it all plays in reverse. Same object,

99
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:36,560
completely different slices, depending only on its angle.

100
00:04:36,590 --> 00:04:39,220
Just show one piece at a time, shadows

101
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:41,850
show everything at once. And a shadow is

102
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:45,040
pure linear algebra. Shine a light straight down

103
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:46,760
on a cube, and the shadow forgets the

104
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:49,940
height. Every point x, y, z lands at

105
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:53,280
x, y. That forgetting is a matrix that

106
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:55,500
keeps x, keeps y, and multiplies z by

107
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:58,980
zero. From four dimensions to three, a three

108
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:01,270
by four matrix keeps x, y, and z

109
00:05:01,270 --> 00:05:04,350
drops w. The straight shadow stacks corners on

110
00:05:04,350 --> 00:05:06,980
top of each other, so we use perspective.

111
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:09,960
The same rule your eyes use, farther things

112
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:13,440
look smaller. Place a camera out along w.

113
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:16,100
Each point is scaled by the camera distance

114
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:19,290
divided by the camera distance minus w. Points

115
00:05:19,290 --> 00:05:22,040
with a large w come closer and grow.

116
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:24,700
Points with a small w move away and

117
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:26,981
shrink. So the eight near corners form a

118
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:29,360
big cube, and the eight far corners form

119
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:32,400
a small cube. Find them along w, and

120
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:35,090
you get the famous picture, a cube inside

121
00:05:35,090 --> 00:05:37,810
a cube. The six lopsided shapes between them

122
00:05:37,810 --> 00:05:41,540
are real cubes, seen in four-dimensional perspective. Now

123
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:44,990
let's make it move. In two dimensions, rotating

124
00:05:44,990 --> 00:05:46,620
by an angle theta is a two by

125
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:49,410
two matrix. The new x is cosine theta

126
00:05:49,410 --> 00:05:52,470
times x minus sine theta times y. The

127
00:05:52,470 --> 00:05:54,860
new y is sine theta times x plus

128
00:05:54,860 --> 00:05:58,100
cosine theta times y. In three dimensions, we

129
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:00,700
say an object rotates around an axis. But

130
00:06:00,700 --> 00:06:03,110
when a cube spins around the vertical axis,

131
00:06:03,110 --> 00:06:05,560
it's height never changes, only the other two

132
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:09,030
coordinates mix. So rotation really happens in a

133
00:06:09,030 --> 00:06:11,450
plane. The axis is just what is left

134
00:06:11,450 --> 00:06:15,190
over. That idea unlocks four dimensions. With three

135
00:06:15,190 --> 00:06:18,860
axes, there are three planes. xy, xz, and

136
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:22,690
yz. With four axes, there are six. xy,

137
00:06:22,690 --> 00:06:28,340
xz, xw, yz, yw, and zw. The formula

138
00:06:28,340 --> 00:06:30,680
is n times n minus 1 divided by

139
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:33,380
2. Four times 3 divided by 2 is

140
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:36,290
six ways to rotate. Let's rotate in the

141
00:06:36,290 --> 00:06:38,800
zw plane. The matrix leaves x and y

142
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:41,120
alone and mixes z with w using the

143
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:44,270
same cosine and sine. Watch the shadow. The

144
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:46,760
inner cube grows, the outer cube shrinks, and

145
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:49,680
they pass through each other. The tesoract seems

146
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:52,680
to turn inside out. But nothing bends. The

147
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:55,940
matrix is orthogonal. This determinant is 1, and

148
00:06:55,940 --> 00:06:58,190
every edge keeps its length. It is a

149
00:06:58,190 --> 00:07:01,000
rigid object, turning in a direction we can

150
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,750
only see as a shadow. Four dimensions even

151
00:07:03,750 --> 00:07:06,250
allow a double rotation. In the xy plane

152
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:08,960
and the zw plane at the same time.

153
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:11,010
No axis is left over, and the only

154
00:07:11,010 --> 00:07:13,760
point that stays still is the very center.

155
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:16,030
So is any of this real? We may

156
00:07:16,030 --> 00:07:18,840
never step into a fourth direction of space,

157
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:22,070
but its mathematics is everywhere. This treats time

158
00:07:22,070 --> 00:07:24,620
as a fourth coordinate of space time. Game

159
00:07:24,620 --> 00:07:27,720
engines and spacecraft use quaternions, numbers with four

160
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:30,830
parts, to rotate objects smoothly. An inside a

161
00:07:30,830 --> 00:07:33,150
model like chat gpt, a single word is

162
00:07:33,150 --> 00:07:36,540
a vector with thousands of dimensions, measured and

163
00:07:36,540 --> 00:07:39,000
projected with the same tools we just used

164
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,600
on a tesoract. That is the real lesson.

165
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:44,860
Your eyes stop at three dimensions. Linear algebra

166
00:07:44,860 --> 00:07:47,830
does not. Let's recap. A dimension is the

167
00:07:47,830 --> 00:07:51,050
number of independent numbers needed to describe a

168
00:07:51,050 --> 00:07:54,620
position. Adding a fourth number creates a fourth

169
00:07:54,620 --> 00:07:57,530
direction. Dragging a cube along it builds a

170
00:07:57,530 --> 00:08:00,640
tesoract with 16 corners, 32 edges and a

171
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:04,030
long diagonal of exactly two. We see it

172
00:08:04,030 --> 00:08:06,530
through slices, like frames of the video, and

173
00:08:06,530 --> 00:08:10,940
through shadows, which are projection matrices. And rotation

174
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:14,550
happens in planes, giving four dimensions six ways

175
00:08:14,550 --> 00:08:16,560
to turn. So the next time you watch

176
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:19,180
a video, remember, you are already sliding through

177
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:22,800
a higher dimension, one frame at a time.

178
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:24,720
If this made the fourth dimension a little

179
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:27,780
less mysterious, subscribe for more complex math made

180
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:29,800
visual. See you in the next dimension!

